'''Shitateruhime''' (下光比売命) is a female deity in {{ill|Japanese mythology|ja|日本神話}} associated with {{ill|Shinto|ja|神道}}. She is also known by the names '''Takahime''' (高比売) and '''Waka-kunitama''' (稚国玉).

== Overview ==

In the ''{{ill|Kojiki|ja|古事記}}'', she is referred to as '''Takahime-no-Mikoto''' (高比売命) and also as '''Shitateruhime-no-Mikoto''' (下光比売命). In the ''{{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|日本書紀}}'', she appears as '''Shitateruhime''' (下照姫) or '''Shitateruhime-no-Mikoto''', and in the ''{{ill|Sendai Kuji Hongi|ja|先代旧事本紀}}'', she is called '''Shitateruhime-no-Mikoto'''.

According to the ''Kojiki'' and the ''Nihon Shoki,'' she married {{ill|Ame-No-Wakahiko|ja|天若日子}}, who had been dispatched from {{ill|Takamagahara|ja|高天原}} to subjugate the {{ill|Ashihara no Nakatsukuni|ja|葦原中国}} (the Central Land of Reed Plains). However, Ame-wakahiko secretly plotted to claim the land for himself. When Ame-wakahiko was killed by a divine arrow sent from Takamagahara, Shitateruhime’s grief-stricken cries reached the heavens. Her mourning led to the descent of Ame-wakahiko's family, including his father {{ill|Amatsukunitama-no-Mikoto|ja|天津国玉神}}, to perform his funeral rites. During this event, her brother {{ill|Ajisukitakahikone|ja|阿遅鉏高日子根神}} was mistaken for the deceased Ame-wakahiko due to their resemblance, leading to a conflict. Shitateruhime composed a lament known as the ''Hina-buri'' (夷振), expressing her sorrow.

== Genealogy ==

[[File:Ohokuninushi family tree.png|thumb|250px|right|Genealogy of Ōkuninushi (''Kojiki''). Blue indicates male deities, red indicates female deities, and yellow indicates unspecified gender.]]

According to the ''Kojiki,'' Shitateruhime is the daughter of {{ill|Ōkuninushi|ja|大国主神}} and {{ill|Takirihime-no-Mikoto|ja|多紀理毘売命}}, making her the sister of {{ill|Ajisukitakahikone|ja|阿遅鉏高日子根神}}. This genealogy is also supported by the ''Sendai Kuji Hongi.''

== Shrines ==

Shitateruhime is enshrined at several locations, including:
* {{ill|Hime Shrine (Toyama)|ja|比賣神社}} in {{ill|Toyama Prefecture|ja|富山県}}, where she is the main deity.
* {{ill|Tamatsuoka Shrine|ja|玉津岡神社}} in {{ill|Kyoto Prefecture|ja|京都府}}, dedicated to her worship.
* {{ill|Himekoso Shrine|ja|比売許曽神社}} in {{ill|Osaka Prefecture|ja|大阪府}}.
* {{ill|Izumo Taisha|ja|出雲大社}}’s external shrine, known as ''Ōanamochi-no-Miko Tamae Shrine'' (大穴持御子玉江神社).

