{{ill|Shitateruhime|lt=Shitateruhime-no-Mikoto|ja|シタテルヒメ|simple|User:Immanuelle/Shitateruhime|en:draft|8=fr|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shitateruhime|qq=}} is a daughter of {{ill|Tagorihime|ja|タキリビメ|simple|User:Immanuelle/Takiribime|en:draft|8=fr|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tagorihime|qq=|comment=enwiki is a redirect}}, one of the three sisters worshipped at [[Munakata Taisha|Munakata Taisha]]

The origins of [[:en:Shitori Shrine (Tottori)|Shitori Shrine]] are unknown. Although there is no documentary evidence, it is believed that it began as the family shrine for the {{Nihongo|Shitori clan|倭文氏}} of [[Kofun period|Kofun period]] craftsmen who weavers, who regarded {{ill|Takehazuchi|lt=Takehazuchi-no-Mikoto|ja|天羽槌雄神|1=Takehazuchi|2=ja|3=天羽槌雄神|4=zh|5=天羽槌雄神|6=fr|7=Takehazuchi|lt=Takehazuchi|simple|User:Immanuelle/Takehazuchi|en:draft|8=fr|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takehazuchi|13=User:Immanuelle/Takehazuchi|qq=}} as their ancestor. However, the shrine's legends are all centered around {{ill|Shitateruhime|lt=Shitateruhime-no-Mikoto|ja|シタテルヒメ|simple|User:Immanuelle/Shitateruhime|en:draft|8=fr|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shitateruhime|qq=}}, whom the shrine legend claims arrived in this location from [[Izumo-taisha|Izumo]] by boat. She established her residence on the mountain behind this shrine, and after her death, she was buried in a ''[[kofun|kofun]]'' constructed in the shrine's precincts.<ref name="Yoshiki">{{cite book |last1=Yoshiki |first1=Emi |title=Zenkoku 'Ichinomiya' tettei gaido |date=2007 |publisher=PHP Institute |isbn=978-4569669304 |language=Japanese}}</ref>

== Overview ==

In the ''[[:en:Kojiki|Kojiki]]'', she is referred to as '''Takahime-no-Mikoto''' (高比売命) and also as '''Shitateruhime-no-Mikoto''' (下光比売命). In the ''[[:en:Nihon Shoki|Nihon Shoki]]'', she appears as '''Shitateruhime''' (下照姫) or '''Shitateruhime-no-Mikoto''', and in the ''[[:en:Kujiki|Sendai Kuji Hongi]]'', she is called '''Shitateruhime-no-Mikoto'''.

According to the ''Kojiki'' and the ''Nihon Shoki,'' she married {{ill|Ame no Wakahiko|ja|天若日子|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ame-No-Wakahiko|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to アメノワカヒコ}}, who had been dispatched from [[:en:Takamagahara|Takamagahara]] to subjugate the [[:en:Ashihara no Nakatsukuni|Ashihara no Nakatsukuni]] (the Central Land of Reed Plains). However, Ame-wakahiko secretly plotted to claim the land for himself. When Ame-wakahiko was killed by a divine arrow sent from Takamagahara, Shitateruhime’s grief-stricken cries reached the heavens. Her mourning led to the descent of Ame-wakahiko's family, including his father {{ill|Amatsukunitama-no-Mikoto|ja|天津国玉神|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Amatsukunitama-no-Mikoto|qq=}}, to perform his funeral rites. During this event, her brother {{ill|Ajisukitakahikone|ja|阿遅鉏高日子根神|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ajisukitakahikone|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to アヂスキタカヒコネ}} was mistaken for the deceased Ame-wakahiko due to their resemblance, leading to a conflict. Shitateruhime composed a lament known as the ''Hina-buri'' (夷振), expressing her sorrow.

